nt :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469654} {:answer/id #uuid "3808bdfa-f327-5c13-b5d5-663088e525e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3808bdfa-f327-5c13-b5d5-663088e525e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469655} :answer-field/id #uuid "b72c82db-3efb-5c9a-9d57-f990f418cde7" :answer-field/key "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc49e39d-dc41-5d92-84ab-b1310e571077" :question/math-academy-id "q-93988" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/id #uuid "268943d4-aaa4-5e39-9db5-48d7523acdc3" :topic/math-academy-id 3118 :topic/title "Symmetric Matrices"}]}] 