answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469672} {:answer/id #uuid "4adc5058-528d-5d28-acec-3db27a965ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f33c3f-be58-5c23-81dd-d2484c6e27b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469672} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b9d8951-1ed6-52a8-9476-b293978d9255" :answer-field/key "$M=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df2ac6ff-519f-58d2-90f1-c2ccb3e51740" :question/math-academy-id "q-94059" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $K$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $L$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since ${m}_{12}\\ne {m}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$K$ and $L$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049450 :topic/id #uuid "f66a029b-c648-5d01-a90b-9ef34d0631d3" :topic/math-