* a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6847dae1-289b-5050-b4ae-95ca49b57e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-94632" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $L$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under function addition” is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & =x+(1-x) \\\\  & =(x-x)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot x=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$” is false, while statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$” is true. $L$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050453 :topic/id #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" :topic/math-academy-id 1907 :topic/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560356} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561087}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #ed