uuid "8c9df35c-9eb8-5209-9845-9e0e1399e294" :question/math-academy-id "q-94862" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ a & 2b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43962068-8fb6-5a57-bb26-9e175e6b0cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452155} {:answer/id #uuid "641f943a-e18f-5d94-9365-0c6f6e083b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43962068-8fb6-5a57-bb26-9e175e6b0cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452155} :answer-field/id #uuid "127d9aba-888f-53a3-99de-2588d9b0359d" :answer-field/key "${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452925} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f9cc51-651a-5dc1-bd46-98fe27b8c6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452157} {:answer/id #uuid "f7234fc6-a58a-5a8e-a4ef-8f59395d460c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text