ne each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89dcded0-192d-5dc2-a345-d0c8cd7c8279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref