6422449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb1a3b53-c6cd-51f3-bcd2-63da1f7e9756" :question/math-academy-id "q-96715" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$ is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}$ is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=3\\sqrt{y}$ is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/title "Optional assessment retake" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569329} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569332}] :task-item/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569341 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360700} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 39.914 :task-item/id #uuid "d536b925-c734-48c8-8e0f-495fa755d9ff" :task-item/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569342} :task-item/responses [{:answer/id #uuid "04341a8f-3a94-579e-8ed0-431601950988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "fa